The `@glowcap/web` package compiles glowcap's protocol core to WebAssembly. A
browser uses the same `Session` operations as the TypeScript binding.
JavaScript supplies the byte channel and scheduling.

# Dial

A page cannot bind a socket, so every path below is a dial. The iroh tab
reaches an iroh node id through a [relay]($link.page('relay')) over `wss://`.
The `noise-relay` tab reaches a peer registered at the relay, and Noise_XK
seals the stream so the relay forwards only ciphertext. `keyed-tcp` and
`keyed-relay` name a key but send CapTP frames in the clear. `tcp` is for
testing.

Install an iroh, Noise-relay, or WebRTC connector for those locators. The
built-in runtime also opens `tcp` and `keyed-tcp` through WebSocket.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:browser GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import { init } from "@glowcap/web";
>
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/glowcap_host.wasm"),
> 	relayUrl: (locator) => `wss://relay.example/?locator=${encodeURIComponent(locator)}`,
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> // `@glowcap/iroh` loads a compiled library through FFI, so a page parses the locator on the web runtime.
> const partner = web.parseLocator("ocapn://<64 lowercase hex characters>.iroh");
> ```
> **webrtc**
> ```ts
> import { connector, locator, type SignalingAuthority } from "@glowcap/webrtc";
> import { init } from "@glowcap/web";
>
> declare const signaling: SignalingAuthority;
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/glowcap_host.wasm"),
> 	connectors: [connector(signaling)],
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> const partner = locator("<64 lowercase hex characters from the peer>");
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```ts
> import { connector, noise } from "@glowcap/relay";
> import { init } from "@glowcap/web";
>
> const relayUrl = "wss://relay.example/relay";
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/glowcap_host.wasm"),
> 	connectors: [connector()],
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> const partner = noise.locator("<64 lowercase hex characters>", relayUrl);
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```ts
> import { keyed } from "@glowcap/tcp";
> import { init } from "@glowcap/web";
>
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/glowcap_host.wasm"),
> 	// A page cannot open raw TCP, so a relay carries the bytes.
> 	wsUrl: (host, port) => `ws://127.0.0.1:9000/?to=${host}:${port}`,
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> // keyed-tcp is cleartext through the relay. Use WebRTC for a sealed browser path.
> const partner = keyed.locator("<64 lowercase hex characters>", "127.0.0.1", 9099);
> ```
> **keyed-relay**
> ```ts
> import { connector, keyed } from "@glowcap/relay";
> import { init } from "@glowcap/web";
>
> const relayUrl = "wss://relay.example/rendezvous";
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/glowcap_host.wasm"),
> 	connectors: [connector()],
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> const partner = keyed.locator("<64 lowercase hex characters>", relayUrl);
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import { locator } from "@glowcap/tcp";
> import { init } from "@glowcap/web";
>
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/glowcap_host.wasm"),
> 	// A page cannot open raw TCP, so a relay carries the bytes.
> 	wsUrl: (host, port) => `ws://127.0.0.1:9000/?to=${host}:${port}`,
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> const partner = locator("127.0.0.1", 9099);
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```ts
await using session = await runtime.connect(partner);
const greeter = await session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability this peer published
const reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
console.log(reply);
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:dial:browser -->
```

When you hold the key and address as separate pieces instead, import
`@glowcap/tcp` before `init` and use `tcp.keyed.locator`. The locator package
does not load a compiled library or depend on the initialized wasm adapter.

`init` instantiates `glowcap_host.wasm` (built by `zig build wasm-driver`) and
hands back the configured browser runtime. From there `connect`, `fetch`,
`send`, `spawn`, and `publish` work as on the
[TypeScript page]($link.page('bindings/ts')).

# Browser dial paths

To reach a **non-browser** peer, the page dials over WebSocket. A host-and-port
dial opens `ws://host:port/` by default, straight to a peer that accepts
WebSocket itself. For a peer that only speaks raw TCP or iroh, dial through a
[relay]($link.page('relay')): `init`'s `wsUrl` option maps a host-and-port dial
to the relay's URL, and `relayUrl` does the same for a locator only a
non-browser peer can serve, such as iroh. The keyed handshake still runs end
to end between the two peers.

To reach **another browser**, open a direct
[WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) DataChannel. Once the channel is up, no
server sits on the byte path. Two browser peers that first met through a relay
can also dial each other cold by identity, which `init`'s `relayUrl` option
enables.

# Next

The [TypeScript page]($link.page('bindings/ts')) is the full API, minus
`listen`. The
[`@glowcap/web` README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/ts/web/README.md)
covers installing the package and serving the wasm.
